### 11.4.13 Scale \\( n = 260 \\): Galactic Scale

**Parameters:**
\\[
\\begin{aligned}
L(260) &= \\ell_P \\varphi^{260} \\approx 2.8 \\times 10^{33} \\, \\text{m} \
\\\\
\\tau(260) &= t_P \\varphi^{260} (1 + 260\\varepsilon) \\approx 1.6 \\times \
10^{17} \\, \\text{s} \\approx 5.1 \\times 10^{9} \\, \\text{years} \\\\
\\delta x(260) &= \\ell_P \\varphi^{-260} \\approx 5.7 \\times 10^{-89} \\, \
\\text{m} \\\\
\\mathcal{C}(260) &= 780 \\ln \\varphi \\approx 380.0 \\\\
\\rho_I(260) &= \\rho_0 \\varphi^{-260} \\approx 1.9 \\times 10^{-63} \\rho_0 \
\\\\
v_{\\text{wave}}(260) &= \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{1.9 \\times 10^{-63} \\rho_0 \\cdot \
\\kappa}} \\cdot \\frac{\\ell_P}{\\tau(260)} \\\\
S_{\\text{stab}}(260) &= 0.9999 \\, \\text{(stable)} \\\\
W(260) &= \\text{Round}(\\varphi^{260}/13) \\approx 3
\\end{aligned}
\\]

**Expanded Equation:** Same form as above, with \\( n = 260 \\) substituted.

**Physical Interpretation:** The galactic scale. This is the domain of \
**galactic dynamics**.

---

### 11.4.14 Scale \\( n = 280 \\): Galaxy Cluster Scale

**Parameters:**
\\[
\\begin{aligned}
L(280) &= \\ell_P \\varphi^{280} \\approx 1.8 \\times 10^{37} \\, \\text{m} \
\\\\
\\tau(280) &= t_P \\varphi^{280} (1 + 280\\varepsilon) \\approx 1.0 \\times \
10^{22} \\, \\text{s} \\approx 3.2 \\times 10^{14} \\, \\text{years} \\\\
\\delta x(280) &= \\ell_P \\varphi^{-280} \\approx 8.8 \\times 10^{-94} \\, \
\\text{m} \\\\
\\mathcal{C}(280) &= 840 \\ln \\varphi \\approx 409.2 \\\\
\\rho_I(280) &= \\rho_0 \\varphi^{-280} \\approx 1.9 \\times 10^{-68} \\rho_0 \
\\\\
v_{\\text{wave}}(280) &= \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{1.9 \\times 10^{-68} \\rho_0 \\cdot \
\\kappa}} \\cdot \\frac{\\ell_P}{\\tau(280)} \\\\
S_{\\text{stab}}(280) &= 0.9999 \\, \\text{(stable)} \\\\
W(280) &= \\text{Round}(\\varphi^{280}/13) \\approx 3
\\end{aligned}
\\]

**Expanded Equation:** Same form as above, with \\( n = 280 \\) substituted.

**Physical Interpretation:** The galaxy cluster scale. This is the domain of \
**large-scale structure**.

---

### 11.4.15 Scale \\( n = 292 \\): Observable Universe

**Parameters:**
\\[
\\begin{aligned}
L(292) &= \\ell_P \\varphi^{292} \\approx 1.7 \\times 10^{40} \\, \\text{m} \
\\\\
\\tau(292) &= t_P \\varphi^{292} (1 + 292\\varepsilon) \\approx 1.0 \\times \
10^{26} \\, \\text{s} \\approx 3.2 \\times 10^{18} \\, \\text{years} \\\\
\\delta x(292) &= \\ell_P \\varphi^{-292} \\approx 9.5 \\times 10^{-98} \\, \
\\text{m} \\\\
\\mathcal{C}(292) &= 876 \\ln \\varphi \\approx 426.8 \\\\
\\rho_I(292) &= \\rho_0 \\varphi^{-292} \\approx 1.9 \\times 10^{-73} \\rho_0 \
\\\\
v_{\\text{wave}}(292) &= \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{1.9 \\times 10^{-73} \\rho_0 \\cdot \
\\kappa}} \\cdot \\frac{\\ell_P}{\\tau(292)} \\\\
S_{\\text{stab}}(292) &= 0.9999 \\, \\text{(stable)} \\\\
W(292) &= \\text{Round}(\\varphi^{292}/13) \\approx 3
\\end{aligned}
\\]

**Expanded Equation:** Same form as above, with \\( n = 292 \\) substituted.

**Physical Interpretation:** The cosmic scale. This is the domain of \
**cosmology**. The phase lag \\( \\tau(292) \\approx 3.2 \\times 10^{18} \\) \
years is approximately the age of the universe.

